A permit shall be issued in discretion of the Chief of the fire department and in accordance with the following guidelines and conditions:

- a. The fire shall be on the premises owned, rented or otherwise under control and care of the individual obtaining the permit;

- b. No burning shall be done when the wind is blowing;

© Materials to be burned shall be racked in piles or cleared ground or a privately owned paved area shall not be closer than seven times the diameter of the pile to any building, fence, structure, uncleared ground or property line or within 50 feet of any building or structure; however, a fire may be kindled and maintained as close as 15 feet to a building or structure if the fire is contained in an approved waste burner.

- d. The larger area of ground covered must be confined by a plowed fire break or a cleared border not less than six feet in width;

- e. No rubber or other materials that will create an offensive odor shall be burned;

ie The burning shall be under the constant supervision of sufficient adults who shall have connected garden hoses or other approved fire extinguishing appliances at hand adequate to extinguish the fire in time;

- g. No person shall initiate or allow to be initiated or maintained a fire before 10:00 a.m. or later than one hour before sunset. All burning shall be stopped by sundown and ashes thoroughly wet down, extinguished or covered with earth to prevent rekindling;

- h. In receiving a burning permit from the city, the person signing the permit accepts full responsibility for any damage that may result from its use.

i The person receiving permit produces evidence of a permit to burn from the Georgia Forestry Commission.

